{"name":"Crystallise","hex":"#ecdfdf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crystallise","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Non-Oxford British English standard spelling of crystallize."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crystallise"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crystallization","blocks":[{"text":"Crystallization is a process that leads to solids with a uniform pattern of atoms or molecules, i.e. a crystal. The uniform nature of a crystalline solid can be contrasted with amorphous solids in which atoms or molecules lack regular organization. Crystallization can occur by various routes including precipitation from solution, freezing of a liquid, or deposition from a gas. Attributes of the resulting crystal can depend largely on factors such as temperature, air pressure, cooling rate, or solute concentration."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ce/Process-of-Crystallization-200px.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystallization"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/SnowflakesWilsonBentley.jpg/500px-SnowflakesWilsonBentley.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/SnowflakesWilsonBentley.jpg/500px-SnowflakesWilsonBentley.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Snowflakes are a very well-known example, where subtle differences in crystal growth conditions result in different geometries.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASnowflakesWilsonBentley.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Crystallized_honey.jpg/500px-Crystallized_honey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Crystallized_honey.jpg/1280px-Crystallized_honey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Crystallized_honey.jpg/500px-Crystallized_honey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Crystallized honey","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrystallized_honey.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/LT-SEM_snow_crystal_magnification_series-3.jpg/500px-LT-SEM_snow_crystal_magnification_series-3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/LT-SEM_snow_crystal_magnification_series-3.jpg/500px-LT-SEM_snow_crystal_magnification_series-3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Low-temperature SEM magnification series for a snow crystal. The crystals are captured, stored, and sputter-coated with platinum at cryo-temperatures for imaging.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALT-SEM_snow_crystal_magnification_series-3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Crystallization_diffraction_TEM.png/500px-Crystallization_diffraction_TEM.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Crystallization_diffraction_TEM.png/1280px-Crystallization_diffraction_TEM.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Crystallization_diffraction_TEM.png/500px-Crystallization_diffraction_TEM.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Time-resolved TEM diffraction patterns of ZrO2 showing the transition from an amorphous to a crystalline structure under electron beam exposure, evidenced by the emergence of Bragg peaks (or disks).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org